BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:6168c5dcff7a08f6@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261223
SUMMARY:In-Rhythm
LOCATION:KNMA\, New Delhi\, Kiran Nadar Museum of Art\, Saket\, New Delhi
  110017
URL:https://www.knma.org/whats-on/exhibitions/bice-lazzari-and-nasreen-mo
 hamedi-in-rhythm
DESCRIPTION:Bice Lazzari and Nasreen Mohamedi\nIn-Rhythm brings together 
 the works of Italian artist Bice Lazzari (1900-1981) and Indian artist Na
 sreen Mohamedi (1937-1990)\, two artists who never met yet developed stri
 kingly resonant approaches to abstraction across different geographies an
 d histories. Working during a period when modernist discourse remained la
 rgely shaped by male experience\, both artists pursued independent visual
  languages grounded in discipline\, repetition and sustained attention. F
 or them\, abstraction was not an escape from reality but a way of engagin
 g with it. Formed within different cultural and social landscapes\, both 
 artists lived through periods of profound historical rupture and pursued 
 abstraction at a time when the structures of modernism remained largely s
 haped by male experience. Bringing together works from 1920s-1930s and 19
 60s to the early 1980s\, the exhibition explores their shared engagement 
 with line\, rhythm\, geometry and restraint. Rather than suggesting influ
 ence\, it reveals an unexpected artistic kinship\, where limitation becom
 es a means of creating clarity\, emotion and freedom. Through subtle vari
 ations in line\, spacing and colour\, both artists transformed repetition
  into something lyrical and deeply personal. Presented by KNMA in partner
 ship with the Italian Cultural Centre\, New Delhi\, the exhibition culmin
 ates in a direct visual dialogue between two singular practices that cont
 inue to reshape our understanding of abstraction in art. The exhibition f
 eatures artworks from the Archivio Bice Lazzari\, Rome. About the artists
  Bice Lazzari Bice Lazzari (1900–1981) was one of the pioneering figure
  of the twentieth century Italian art. She devoted her entire life to a f
 ield that was not considered appropriate for a woman at the time. Trained
  as a young girl in Classical piano\, and later at the Accademia di Belle
  Arti in Venice in 1916\, she began her artistic career by working on the
  loom\, tapestry and applied art. She collaborated closely with Giò Pont
 i and Carlo Scarpa. Towards 1930s\, she engaged more with avant-garde abs
 traction and became a forerunner of conceptual painting. She went on to p
 roduce important works on canvas and paper\, ranging from figurative work
 s to experiments in architecturally inspired geometric abstraction reveal
 ing her nuanced exploration of minimal forms\, line\, space and repetitio
 n. Nasreen Mohamedi Nasreen Mohamedi (1937–1990) was an Indian artist\,
  born in Karachi and raised in Mumbai. She is regarded as an important fi
 gure within Indian modernism\, particularly for her distinct approach to 
 abstraction. After studying at Central Saint Martins in London\, she trav
 elled and worked in Europe before returning to India in the early 1970s. 
 She later joined the Faculty of Fine Arts at MS University\, Baroda\, whe
 re she taught and developed her practice. Her visual language is defined 
 by minimal lines\, geometric forms\, grids\, rhythm and space. Photograph
 y also formed an important part of her practice. Influenced by poetry\, I
 ndian classical music and modernist architecture\, she reduced forms to t
 heir essential rhythms and structures.\n\nhttps://www.knma.org/whats-on/e
 xhibitions/bice-lazzari-and-nasreen-mohamedi-in-rhythm
CATEGORIES:exhibition\,art
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2bd525302f6f73c4@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261223
SUMMARY:Inheritors of Earth
LOCATION:KNMA\, New Delhi\, Kiran Nadar Museum of Art\, Saket\, New Delhi
  110017
URL:https://www.knma.org/whats-on/exhibitions/inheritors-of-earth
DESCRIPTION:Inheritors of Earth \, curated by Premjish Achari\, invites v
 iewers into the relational world of plants\, entities long treated as bac
 kdrop\, resource\, or specimen rather than co-inhabitants of the earth. T
 he title draws on Vaikom Muhammad Basheer’s Bhumiyude Avakashikal \, en
 visioning humans\, flora and fauna as equal co-custodians of the planet\,
  and on ecological activist Kallen Pokkudan\, whose mangrove-planting wor
 k fused Dalit history with ecological repair. Through paintings\, photogr
 aphs\, installations and video\, artists here practice a “phytosensitiv
 e” approach: encountering rather than merely representing plants\, trac
 ing colonial botanical migrations\, and honoring uncredited lower-caste k
 nowledge-keepers erased from scientific archives. Rejecting both extracti
 on and exoticism\, the exhibition asks viewers to look with plants\, not 
 just at them\, moving from usefulness and utility toward humility\, kinsh
 ip\, and coexistence—imagining a future shaped by plant life on its own
  terms. The exhibition is proud to host a set of artworks from the Shared
  Ecologies archive as part of an extended reading room. Ben Goldfarb has 
 designed a special soundscape for the exhibition. Independent curator and
  researcher Oorja Garg and IMPULSE Magazine\, New York are leading a spec
 ial reading group for the duration of the exhibition.\n\nhttps://www.knma
 .org/whats-on/exhibitions/inheritors-of-earth
CATEGORIES:exhibition\,art\,history\,nature\,sociology
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d7f6948c32ae33f4@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260923
SUMMARY:Standing on Sacred Ground
LOCATION:Bikaner House\, Pandara Road\, India Gate\, New Delhi 110011
URL:https://bikanerhouse.rajasthan.gov.in/upcoming-events/2026/9
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Kalakriti Art Gallery\, Hyderabad\, in this grou
 p exhibition the artists have examined the shifts of the recent past thro
 ugh the lens of their own visual vocabulary. This could range from sudden
  presence or sudden lack of colour\; moving from big to small canvases or
  from figuration to abstraction. A set of artists in this group can be se
 en archiving family histories some\, having noticed the lack of represent
 ation of caste/gender\, are rectifying that by inserting figures/archival
  material into their work. The constant presence of memories of a loved o
 ne\, lost in the recent past\, is being memorialized through found object
 s. \nThe exhibition also looks at personal awakenings and has been curate
 d by Avantika Bhuniya.\n\nhttps://bikanerhouse.rajasthan.gov.in/upcoming-
 events/2026/9
CATEGORIES:exhibition\,art
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bb23c435c57ad5e6@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260922
SUMMARY:Tap Aur Tapish
LOCATION:Bikaner House\, Pandara Road\, India Gate\, New Delhi 110011
URL:https://bikanerhouse.rajasthan.gov.in/upcoming-events/2026/9
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Art Explore\, this exhibition serves as a powerf
 ul platform to present Vipul Kumar’s artistic journey — from his earl
 y stone sculptures to his transformative engagement with clay. Central to
  the show are works that explore environmental fragility\, spiritual intr
 ospection\, and mythological symbolism. Drawing from Jain and Buddhist co
 smologies\, as well as poetic traditions such as that of Kabir\, Kumar’
 s sculptures evoke a timeless quality while remaining deeply rooted in co
 ntemporary concerns. The exhibition will include key pieces and newly dev
 eloped works that mark a turning point in his career. With raw\, weathere
 d surfaces and organic\, monumental forms\, the sculptures invite viewers
  into a meditative space where philosophical inquiry meets sensory experi
 ence. This proposed solo exhibition highlights Vipul Kumar’s mastery ov
 er material and form and asserts his pivotal role in shaping contemporary
  Indian ceramics. It reaffirms ceramics as a serious medium of artistic e
 xpression and brings critical and curatorial attention to an artist who c
 ontinues to challenge boundaries with quiet intensity and profound clarit
 y.\n\nhttps://bikanerhouse.rajasthan.gov.in/upcoming-events/2026/9
CATEGORIES:exhibition\,art
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:de7824e340f3a2f5@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Oriental Scenary
LOCATION:Bikaner House\, Pandara Road\, India Gate\, New Delhi 110011
URL:https://bikanerhouse.rajasthan.gov.in/upcoming-events/2026/9
DESCRIPTION:This exhibition brings together rare maps of India to reveal 
 how cartography functioned as an instrument of power—shaping\, controll
 ing\, and continually reimagining the subcontinent. The exhibition traces
  the evolution of cartographic practices in India from the sixteenth cent
 ury onwards\, beginning with early European attempts to visualize the sub
 continent and extending through colonial surveys to modern printed maps. 
 Initially conceived as instruments of navigation\, conquest and imperial 
 control\, these maps reveal how knowledge of territory was systematically
  produced and codified. This exhibition is curated by Swapna Liddle.\n\nh
 ttps://bikanerhouse.rajasthan.gov.in/upcoming-events/2026/9
CATEGORIES:exhibition\,art
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:6a02433e4ad71f7d@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART:20260916T053000Z
DTEND:20261001T133000Z
SUMMARY:Phulkari: A Lingering Presence By Sunaina Suneja
LOCATION:Art Gallery\, Kamaladevi Complex\, IIC\, India International Cen
 tre\, 40 Max Mueller Marg\, Lodhi Gardens\, New Delhi 110003
URL:https://iicdelhi.in/programmes/exhibition-phulkari-lingering-presence
 -sunaina-suneja
DESCRIPTION:Phulkari: A Lingering Presence\nBy Sunaina Suneja\nPhulkari\,
  the vibrant embroidery tradition of Punjab\, evokes nostalgic memories o
 f color\, music\, dance and poetry. The exhibition traces the journey of 
 reviving the thread-counted Phulkari embroidery and its iconic Pehchaan m
 otif\, celebrating Punjabi womanhood through a rich collection of vintage
  heirlooms alongside contemporary works.\nThe exhibition will be inaugura
 ted on Wednesday 16 September at 11:30 hrs\nA walkthrough by Sunaina Sune
 ja will be conducted on Friday\, September 18 & 25\, Saturday\, September
  19 & 26 at 16:00 hrs.\nA session titled “We Are All Daughters” invit
 ing participants to bring a treasured object associated with a mother\, g
 randmother or another significant woman in their lives\, and share the me
 mories it holds will be on Wednesday\, 23 September at 16:00 hrs.\nThe ex
 hibition will remain open daily from 11:00 to 19:00 hrs\n\nhttps://iicdel
 hi.in/programmes/exhibition-phulkari-lingering-presence-sunaina-suneja
CATEGORIES:exhibition\,art
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:357ca1dfe97c50a3@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T105600Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260917
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260922
SUMMARY:Visible Vapour
LOCATION:Bikaner House\, Pandara Road\, India Gate\, New Delhi 110011
URL:https://bikanerhouse.rajasthan.gov.in/upcoming-events/2026/9
DESCRIPTION:Shree Naik is a Delhi-based landscape architect and artist wh
 ose creative journey beautifully blends design and fine art. Known for hi
 s exceptional versatility and ambidextrous talent\, he moves effortlessly
  between landscape architecture\, painting\, sketching\, and visual expre
 ssion. Inspired by nature and everyday experiences\, his works reflect a 
 keen eye for detail\, composition\, and emotion. This exhibition celebrat
 es his 50th birthday\, marking five decades of creativity\, exploration\,
  and artistic passion.\n\nhttps://bikanerhouse.rajasthan.gov.in/upcoming-
 events/2026/9
CATEGORIES:exhibition\,art
END:VEVENT
